{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"=== PART 2: the close was REVERSED, and the reason changes how this epic must finish ===\n\nThe epic was closed, then reopened on the operator's rule that closing without completion\nverification is prohibited. Debugging the Bedrock error proved that rule right for a reason\nnobody had seen: **the verification behind the earlier PASS was blind.**\n\n    same command, same ticket, same instance — only the max_tokens clamp differs\n    BEFORE  128 provider 400s, 32 of 32 epic-bug-screen calls FAILED  ->  verdict PASS\n    AFTER     0 provider 400s,  0 failures                            ->  verdict FAIL (exit 1)\n\n--- THREE ROOT CAUSES, each explaining distinct symptoms ---\n 1. **max_tokens is never re-clamped.** `max_output_cfg` only ever RAISES, so a cap set for the\n    128K verifier rides into a call resolving to the 64K trivial model. Anthropic tolerated it;\n    Bedrock rejects it, so `d2ce` (default local dev to Bedrock) surfaced it. FIXED — Gerrit 1361,\n    clamped at `build_model_settings` where the model is known and the 400 originates, applied\n    only to models the ceiling table knows (clamping an UNMAPPED model to the 16000 fallback would\n    shrink a deliberate operator floor — a worse regression than the bug).\n 2. **The screen degrades open.** `epic_bug_screen._row` turns a failed screen into \"unrelated\".\n    Defensible per candidate; when all 32 fail it silently makes the caused_by bug floor a no-op\n    with NO aggregate signal. RECORDED, NOT FIXED — the remedy changes when an epic close blocks,\n    which is an operator ruling.\n 3. **The missing attestation was INTENDED.** `certifiable = not uncertified` — two uncertified\n    descendants withheld the signature, documented at the line. **My original framing of ticket\n    1019 as a \"fail-open gate\" was WRONG and is corrected on that ticket.**\n\n--- WHAT THE GATE SAYS NOW THAT IT CAN SEE ---\nFAIL, on two legitimate findings: AC4 has no real attestation (my independent AC verification pass\nflagged the identical gap separately — two independent checks agreeing), and open bug 2e47 indicts\nthe subtree. 2e47 is the capability map's own output and was invisible for as long as the screen\nwas blind.\n\n--- STATE HANDED OVER ---\n * epic e369: **in_progress, claimed**, 45 descendants, 6 open, **0 uncertified**\n * 0059 + 2bc7 REOPENED — they were the two uncertified closes; re-close each on its OWN merits\n * 94d0 CLOSED — credentials rotated + history scrubbed (operator-attested); the two systemic\n   items carried forward to 2a76, not dropped\n * Gerrit open: 1361 (max_tokens fix, LLM-Review +1), 1359 (the probe)\n\n--- METHOD FINDINGS, PART 2 ---\n 1. **I closed 39c1 on the wrong evidence and reopened it.** AC1 demanded a PASS-LEVEL assertion; I\n    ticked it from a cell that calls `set_parent` directly — having refused that exact substitution\n    hours earlier. Wrote the real cell; it passed live. Adjacent-green substitution is not a thing\n    you learn once.\n 2. **An investigation that refutes its own hypothesis is worth more than one that confirms it.**\n    The 37e7 trace killed its own peer-evidence theory and found 339a instead — a defect that made\n    88d9's parent-clear unreachable in ANY bidirectional pass while its unit suite stayed green,\n    because the one outbound-aware cell FABRICATED its input.\n 3. **A prediction you cannot test is not a refutation.** The 339a trace predicted a `RECON:`\n    breadcrumb. It was absent from CI — and so were ALL `RECON:` lines, because the harness captures\n    pass stderr and prints it only on assertion. Inconclusive, not negative.\n 4. **Two subagents reported contradictions instead of massaging them** — one that my brief\n    predicted the wrong casualty, one that the mechanism refuted its own premise. Both were right.\n 5. **Gates caught two of my own process misses**: a 289-line change with no `file_impact` +\n    plan-review (bugfix-size-attestation), and a new workflow unregistered in the locked naming\n    table. Both correct findings; the sequence was mine to get right.\n 6. **A new `workflow_dispatch` workflow cannot be dispatched until it lands on the DEFAULT branch**\n    — unlike a harness run, which reuses an already-landed workflow on a topic branch."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785860522760183001,"uuid":"890b8528-cfc5-4be4-8417-8a60f711d1e1"}